 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Based on the latest air quality readings for Kuching from the Malaysian Department of Environment (DOE) and real-time tracking stations, **caution is advised, especially for vigorous exercise or sensitive individuals.**\n\n### Current Air Quality Overview\n* **Overall Air Pollutant Index (API):** **71–76 (Moderate)**\n* **Fine Particulate Matter (PM2.5 AQI):** **85–108 (Moderate to Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups)**\n\nWhile the overall 24-hour average API sits in the \"Moderate\" bracket, real-time levels of fine particulate matter (PM2.5)—the primary harmful component of haze—have been fluctuating around the threshold for **Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups (100+)**. \n\n### Is it safe to cycle?\nWhen cycling, your breathing rate and depth increase significantly compared to walking, meaning you inhale a much higher volume of fine particles deep into your lungs.\n\n* **For Healthy Adults:** A **short, low-intensity (casual) ride** is generally acceptable for most healthy individuals. However, you should avoid prolonged endurance rides, sprints, or high-intensity training outdoors while PM2.5 levels are elevated near 100.\n* **For Sensitive Groups:** If you have asthma, allergies, sinus issues, or any underlying heart or lung conditions, **it is best to skip the outdoor ride** and use an indoor trainer instead.\n\n### Tips if You Decide to Ride\n1. **Keep the effort light:** Ride at a conversational pace so you are breathing primarily through your nose (which filters out some larger particles) rather than gasping through your mouth.\n2. **Avoid high-traffic corridors:** Vehicle exhaust combined with background haze creates localized pockets of much worse air quality. Stick to quieter residential routes or parks.\n3. **Listen to your body:** If you notice a scratchy throat, coughing, stinging eyes, or unusual shortness of breath, cut the ride short and head indoors.\n4. **Check live readings before heading out:** Haze levels can shift quickly with wind changes. Check the official **MyEQMS (APIMS)** portal or **AQICN Kuching** station right before you leave—if the PM2.5 reading climbs firmly above 100, opt for an indoor workout.",
